From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 23:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] input: s6sy761: Restart a chip that reports no boot

The driver enables the two supplies and then expects to read the event the
chip posts once it has booted.  That only works when enabling the supplies
actually starts the chip.  Where the rails are always on, described in the
device tree as fixed regulators because whatever drives them is not
reachable yet, the enable changes nothing, the chip has been running since
the bootloader, and the read returns whatever was left on its event stack.
Probe then fails with -ENODEV on hardware that works.

The chip can restart itself on command, and that ends in the same event.
Measured on a Galaxy S10+: reading one event returns 1d 61 02 02 03 00 00
1f, a stale vendor event, and probe gives up.  After a 0x12 written to the
chip the same read returns 09 00 40 00 00 00 00 00, which is an info event
carrying a boot-complete code.  Also measured: 20 ms after the command the
chip NAKs, 50 ms is enough, so the delay already used after a power on is
reused here.

So ask for a restart when, and only when, the event is not the one
expected. A chip whose rails really were switched answers the first read
and never sees the command.
Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c | 46 ++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
index 0f24a9b7306..5072d3f67e7 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 /* commands */
 #define S6SY761_SENSE_ON		0x10
 #define S6SY761_SENSE_OFF		0x11
+#define S6SY761_SW_RESET		0x12
 #define S6SY761_TOUCH_FUNCTION		0x30 /* R/W for get/set */
 #define S6SY761_FIRMWARE_INTEGRITY	0x21
 #define S6SY761_PANEL_INFO		0x23
@@ -288,20 +289,12 @@ static struct attribute *s6sy761_sysfs_attrs[] = {
 };
 ATTRIBUTE_GROUPS(s6sy761_sysfs);
 
-static int s6sy761_power_on(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
+static int s6sy761_boot_event(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
 {
 	u8 buffer[S6SY761_EVENT_SIZE];
 	u8 event;
 	int ret;
 
-	ret = regulator_bulk_enable(ARRAY_SIZE(sdata->regulators),
-				    sdata->regulators);
-	if (ret)
-		return ret;
-
-	msleep(140);
-
-	/* double check whether the touch is functional */
 	ret = i2c_smbus_read_i2c_block_data(sdata->client,
 					    S6SY761_READ_ONE_EVENT,
 					    S6SY761_EVENT_SIZE,
@@ -313,9 +306,42 @@ static int s6sy761_power_on(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
 
 	if ((event != S6SY761_EVENT_INFO &&
 	     event != S6SY761_EVENT_VENDOR_INFO) ||
-	    buffer[1] != S6SY761_INFO_BOOT_COMPLETE) {
+	    buffer[1] != S6SY761_INFO_BOOT_COMPLETE)
 		return -ENODEV;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int s6sy761_power_on(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = regulator_bulk_enable(ARRAY_SIZE(sdata->regulators),
+				    sdata->regulators);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	msleep(140);
+
+	/* double check whether the touch is functional */
+	ret = s6sy761_boot_event(sdata);
+	if (ret == -ENODEV) {
+		/*
+		 * The rails can be always on, and then the enable above
+		 * restarts nothing and there is no boot event to read, just
+		 * whatever was left on the event stack.  Ask the chip to
+		 * restart itself instead, which ends the same way.
+		 */
+		ret = i2c_smbus_write_byte(sdata->client, S6SY761_SW_RESET);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+
+		msleep(140);
+
+		ret = s6sy761_boot_event(sdata);
 	}
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
 
 	ret = i2c_smbus_read_byte_data(sdata->client, S6SY761_BOOT_STATUS);
 	if (ret < 0)
